  "question": {
    "text": "$\\quad \\operatorname{Let} f(x)=\\left(x^{5}-1\\right)\\left(x^{3}+1\\right), g(x)=\\left(x^{2}-1\\right)\\left(x^{2}-x+1\\right)$\r\n\r\nand let $\\mathrm{h}(\\mathrm{x})$ be such that $\\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})=\\mathrm{g}(\\mathrm{x}) \\mathrm{h}(\\mathrm{x})$. Then\r\n\r\n$\\operatorname{limh}_{x \\rightarrow 1} \\mathrm{~h}(\\mathrm{x})$ is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "0",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "1",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "3",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "5",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given $f(x)=g(x) h(x)$\r\n\r\n$\\Rightarrow h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}$\r\n\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\lim _{x \\rightarrow 1} h(x)=\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 1} \\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}$\r\n\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\lim _{x \\rightarrow 1} \\frac{\\left(x^{5}-1\\right)\\left(x^{3}+1\\right)}{\\left(x^{2}-1\\right)\\left(x^{2}-x+1\\right)}$\r\n\r\n$=\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 1} \\frac{x^{5}-1^{5}}{x-1}=5 \\times 1^{4}=5$"
  }
}